**Claude Code Mods are TypeScript event handlers that intercept any tool call, permission prompt, or UI draw event — you can observe, rewrite, or replace the behavior entirely.**

A mod is a small JS/TS file that registers hooks on Claude Code's internal events. Each time Claude calls a tool, asks for permission, or renders part of the interface, it fires an event. Your hook runs alongside it or in its place.

**Three hook modes:**
- **Observe** — read what happened, let it continue unchanged
- **Rewrite** — change the event before it continues (edit the tool call arguments, modify the output)
- **Answer** — handle the event yourself; the normal behavior doesn't run

Hooks form a middleware chain. `next(e)` passes to the next handler; skip it to intercept entirely.

**Minimal mod structure (three files):**

```
my-mod/
  plugin.json   # plugin manifest
  hooks.json    # declares which events your module handles
  register.js   # your event handlers
```

```js
// register.js — block any git push not to a feature branch
export function register({ on }) {
  on('tool:call', async (e, next) => {
    if (e.tool === 'Bash' && e.input?.command?.startsWith('git push')) {
      if (!e.input.command.includes('origin feature/')) {
        return { error: 'Pushes to non-feature branches require manual confirmation.' };
      }
    }
    return next(e);
  });
}
```

**Three things you can build immediately:**
1. **Secret redaction** — hook `tool:result` and scrub any line matching `sk-`, `AKIA`, or your own patterns before Claude reads it
2. **Push gate** — intercept `Bash` calls starting with `git push` and require a branch check
3. **Inline Mermaid** — hook `interface:draw` and replace a code block tagged `mermaid` with a rendered diagram panel

**To install or write:** run `/plugin` in the Claude Code CLI to install a plugin that includes mods. To write your own, create a plugin directory, add `hooks.json` pointing to your code file, and export a `register` function.

**Real limits:**
- Mods run only in the Claude Code CLI and desktop app — they don't run in remote sessions, CI pipelines, or the Agent SDK
- Hooks run synchronously inside Claude Code's event loop; a blocking operation stalls the whole session
- You can't currently register new slash commands or directly modify the system prompt through a mod (use CLAUDE.md for that)
- Mod code runs with the same filesystem and network access as the Claude Code session — vet what you install from the directory

**Sources:** [Mods overview — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/plugins/mods/overview) · [Create a mod — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/plugins/mods/create) · [Customize Claude Code with mods in TypeScript — claude.com/blog](https://claude.com/blog/claude-code-mods) · [Claude Code mods rewrite prompts and approve tool permissions — Runtime Wire](https://runtimewire.com/article/anthropic-claude-code-mods-typescript-permissions)

**Clef returns fast typed probabilities for a fixed schema of routing questions — no generation, no parsing — as a zero-config binding on Cloudflare Workers AI.**

TypeSafe's Jev ([covered September 21](/ai-news/2026-09-21-08-jev-typesafe-decision-model-agent-routing)) introduced the category of specialist decision models for agent routing. Cloudflare's Clef, released today, adds two things Jev doesn't have: open weights (Apache 2.0, available on HuggingFace) and an RL fine-tuning platform so you can adapt the routing schema to your own data.

**Call pattern:**

```javascript
const result = await env.AI.run("@cf/cloudflare/clef", {
  state: "Checkout failing for all customers for the past hour.",
  questions: {
    urgent: { type: "noul", instructions: "Is this urgent?" },
    team: {
      type: "choice",
      instructions: "Which team handles this?",
      criteria: {
        billing: "Payments, invoices, refunds",
        technical: "Outages, errors, configuration",
        sales: "Plans and upgrades"
      }
    }
  }
});
// result: { urgent: { yes: 0.96 }, team: { technical: 0.91, billing: 0.06, sales: 0.03 } }
```

Two models: Clef (27B) and Clef-flash (9B). Cloudflare benchmarks Clef at 2.5× faster than Jev at the median on their classification tasks — vendor figures, no independent replication yet.

**Real limits:** the RL fine-tuning platform requires a paid Cloudflare Workers plan; the decision schema must be fixed upfront — Clef doesn't handle open-ended questions where the answer space is unknown; Workers AI only (no local self-hosting despite Apache 2.0 weights on HuggingFace).

**Why it matters:** working on Cloudflare Workers already gets you a zero-config agent routing layer; the open Apache 2.0 weights give everyone an alternative to proprietary routing models.

**Sources:** [Introducing Clef — Cloudflare Blog](https://blog.cloudflare.com/clef-decision-models/) · [Clef Workers AI docs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/clef/) · [Cloudflare Clef resource page](https://www.cloudflare.com/resource/clef-rl-interest/)

**When Claude returns multiple `tool_use` blocks in one turn, those calls are independent. Run them in parallel with `asyncio.gather` (Python) or `Promise.all` (JS), and return every result in one `user` message. A serial loop leaves speed on the table and can break the protocol.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Detect parallel tool calls in a Claude response, dispatch them concurrently, and return all results correctly — and know how to disable the behavior when sequential execution is required.

**Three takeaways:**
- Claude signals independent calls by including multiple `tool_use` content blocks in a single assistant message; you dispatch them all before the next model turn
- You must return a `tool_result` for every `tool_use` block in the preceding assistant message — omitting one (even for a call you skipped) violates the protocol
- Disable with `tool_choice={"type": "auto", "disable_parallel_tool_use": True}` when your tools have side effects that must execute in order

**The default behavior**

When Claude determines that several tool calls are independent — "look up the weather in Paris AND the exchange rate for EUR/USD" — it returns both as separate `tool_use` blocks in the same assistant message:

```json
{
  "role": "assistant",
  "content": [
    {"type": "tool_use", "id": "tu_01", "name": "get_weather", "input": {"city": "Paris"}},
    {"type": "tool_use", "id": "tu_02", "name": "get_rate", "input": {"pair": "EUR/USD"}}
  ]
}
```

A loop that processes these one at a time loses the parallelism entirely and blocks the slower call behind the faster one.

**The parallel pattern**

```python
import asyncio

async def call_tool(tool_use):
    # dispatch to your actual tool implementation
    name = tool_use.name
    args = tool_use.input
    try:
        result = await your_tool_registry[name](**args)
        return {"type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": tool_use.id, "content": str(result)}
    except Exception as e:
        return {"type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": tool_use.id, "content": str(e), "is_error": True}

# After receiving an assistant message with tool_use blocks:
tool_uses = [block for block in response.content if block.type == "tool_use"]

# Run all calls concurrently
results = await asyncio.gather(*[call_tool(tu) for tu in tool_uses])

# Return ALL results in ONE user message
messages.append({
    "role": "user",
    "content": list(results)
})
```

In JavaScript with the SDK:

```typescript
const toolUses = response.content.filter(b => b.type === "tool_use");
const results = await Promise.all(toolUses.map(async (tu) => {
  try {
    const result = await toolRegistry[tu.name](tu.input);
    return { type: "tool_result", tool_use_id: tu.id, content: String(result) };
  } catch (e) {
    return { type: "tool_result", tool_use_id: tu.id, content: String(e), is_error: true };
  }
}));
messages.push({ role: "user", content: results });
```

**The protocol rule you cannot break**

Every `tool_use` block in the preceding assistant message must have a matching `tool_result` in the next user message. If you skip a call — because you decided not to execute it, because it timed out, because it was for a tool you don't recognize — you still return a result block for it with `is_error: true` and an explanation:

```python
return {
    "type": "tool_result",
    "tool_use_id": tool_use.id,
    "content": "Tool not executed: unsupported in this context",
    "is_error": True
}
```

Omitting a `tool_result` entirely causes the API to reject the next request with a validation error.

**Disabling parallel calls**

When your tools have ordering constraints — writing a record before reading it back, or making an API call that must complete before a dependent call — disable parallel tool use:

```python
response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    tools=[...],
    tool_choice={"type": "auto", "disable_parallel_tool_use": True},
    messages=messages
)
```

Claude will still call multiple tools across turns, but each assistant message will contain at most one `tool_use` block. This is the right default for tools with write side effects or strict ordering requirements.

**Further reading:** [Anthropic parallel tool use docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/parallel-tool-use) covers the full API; the [Tool Runner in the Claude Agent SDK](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/claude-code/sdk) handles this automatically if you're using the managed agentic loop.

**A flat hex dump tells Claude your palette but not your semantic intent; a DTCG primitive→semantic→component file gives it the alias chain it needs to generate code that matches your design system — not just its defaults.**

When you upload a design file to Claude Design, it works with whatever token structure you provide. The problem: a flat list of CSS variables — `--blue-500: #3b82f6` — tells Claude a value but nothing about its role. Generated components end up using arbitrary primitives instead of your semantic tokens, and when your brand colors change, you update one token and the generated code doesn't follow.

The [W3C Design Tokens Community Group (DTCG)](https://tr.designtokens.org/format/) format solves this by expressing meaning, not just values, through three explicit layers:

```json
{
  "color": {
    "primitive": {
      "blue-500": { "$value": "#3b82f6", "$type": "color" }
    },
    "semantic": {
      "interactive-primary": {
        "$value": "{color.primitive.blue-500}",
        "$type": "color",
        "$description": "Default background for interactive CTAs"
      }
    },
    "component": {
      "button-background": {
        "$value": "{color.semantic.interactive-primary}",
        "$type": "color"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Claude reads the alias chain (`button-background` → `interactive-primary` → `blue-500`) and knows to reference semantic tokens in generated code — not primitives. When `interactive-primary` changes, every component that references it updates without touching raw hex values.

**How to feed this to Claude Design:** save your token file as `tokens.json`, then pass it at session start — either paste it into the `/design` prompt or upload it in the Claude Design web app under **Design System → Upload**. Claude resolves aliases before rendering artboards, so `interactive-primary` appears as a named token in the color picker rather than a raw hex.

**Generating the file.** If your tokens already live in Figma variables or a Storybook config, the [gregorymm/design-tokens Claude Code plugin](https://github.com/gregorymm/design-tokens) reads your existing setup and writes a DTCG-compliant `tokens.json` with all three layers. Run it once against your repo; the output is a file you commit and maintain.

**Limits:** tokens must be provided at the start of a Design session. If you start without them and add them mid-conversation, Claude won't retroactively update already-generated artboards — you'll need to restart. The alias chain also breaks silently if a `$value` references a key that doesn't exist in the same file; validate with `npx @tokens-studio/sd-transforms validate tokens.json` before uploading.

**Sources:** [W3C DTCG format specification](https://tr.designtokens.org/format/) · [gregorymm/design-tokens — Claude Code plugin](https://github.com/gregorymm/design-tokens) · [Claude Code Design Token Automation — claudecodeguides.com](https://claudecodeguides.com/claude-code-design-token-automation-from-figma-variables/)

**axe-core + Playwright surfaces the three accessibility gaps generated UI almost always has; the structured violations JSON is exact enough for Claude Code to locate and fix each node automatically.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Run a single Playwright test against any component from a design tool, get structured WCAG violations with exact DOM selectors, and hand the list to Claude Code to fix.

**Three things to know before you start:**

- Generated components reliably fail three WCAG AA checks: icon-only buttons without `aria-label`, helper or placeholder text below 4.5:1 contrast, and pages missing landmark regions (`<main>`, `<nav>`). None look obviously wrong in a browser.
- `@axe-core/playwright` returns violations as structured JSON with `id`, `impact`, `nodes`, and `failureSummary` — exact enough for Claude Code to locate and patch each failing element.
- Add this test once and it runs in CI on every subsequent push, catching regressions after every change rather than only after a design handoff.

**Setup:**

```bash
pnpm add -D @axe-core/playwright
```

**The test:**

```ts
// tests/a11y.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import AxeBuilder from "@axe-core/playwright";
import { writeFileSync } from "fs";

test.describe("Accessibility: generated component", () => {
  test("meets WCAG 2.1 AA", async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto("/dashboard");   // replace with your component route

    const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
      .withTags(["wcag2a", "wcag2aa", "wcag21aa"])
      .analyze();

    // Write violations so Claude Code can read and fix them
    if (results.violations.length > 0) {
      writeFileSync(
        "a11y-violations.json",
        JSON.stringify(results.violations, null, 2)
      );
    }

    expect(results.violations).toHaveLength(0);
  });
});
```

**Handing off to Claude Code:**

When violations exist, `a11y-violations.json` contains the full finding tree: violation rule id, impact level (`critical`, `serious`, `moderate`), and the `target` selector array for each failing node. Give it to Claude Code:

```
Read a11y-violations.json and fix each violation in the corresponding
component file. Use the target selector to locate each failing element.
Do not change layout or visual styling beyond what the fix requires.
```

Claude Code handles the three common gaps reliably: it adds `aria-label` to icon buttons, adjusts text and background color tokens to meet the 4.5:1 contrast ratio, and wraps page content in a `<main>` landmark.

**The constraint to know:** axe-core catches approximately 30–40% of WCAG issues — only those it can verify mechanically against the rendered DOM. Focus order, alt-text relevance, ARIA role plausibility on custom components, and keyboard interaction patterns require a human review pass. Plan one human pass on new generated components; axe-core handles regression detection on every subsequent change.

**Sources:** [Accessibility testing — Playwright docs](https://playwright.dev/docs/accessibility-testing) · [axe-core — Deque Systems](https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core) · [AI accessibility testing tools 2026 — QASkills](https://qaskills.sh/blog/ai-accessibility-testing-tools-2026) (independent audit of automated tool coverage rates)

**Claude Code's TypeScript "function hooks" (Claude Mods) sit behind a flag and replace shell commands with Express/Koa-style middleware: you wrap the engine operation instead of reacting after it.**

**Shell hooks react. Function hooks wrap.** The existing shell hooks (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, etc.) execute a command and get back an allow/deny signal. A function hook takes `($, e, next)` — where `e` is the full typed tool event — and you decide what happens before, during, or instead of the operation.

**Enabling it.**

```bash
CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS=1 claude
```

Or add it permanently under the `env` key in `~/.claude/settings.json`. Without the flag, your module file is silently ignored.

**Writing one.** A function hook lives in a plugin directory (`.claude/plugins/my-plugin/`), alongside `hooks/hooks.json`:

```json
{ "modules": ["./hooks.ts"] }
```

Then in `hooks.ts`:

```typescript
import type { HookModule } from 'claude-code';

export const onBashToolUse: HookModule['onBashToolUse'] = async ($, e, next) => {
  if (e.input.command.startsWith('rm -rf')) {
    return { type: 'deny', reason: 'Destructive command blocked' };
  }
  const result = await next();
  $.ui.status(`Exit code: ${result.exitCode}`);
  return result;
};
```

Run `/plugin-types .claude/plugins/my-plugin/` to generate `claude-code.d.ts` with full IDE autocompletion for `$` and every event type.

**What wrapping unlocks.** A function hook can accumulate state across tool calls via `$.store`, draw ephemeral status lines with `$.ui.status()`, short-circuit with a cached result when the same file-read recurs, or rewrite `e.input` before the engine acts — all in one function, without a pre/post pair.

**Limits.** Early access: `CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS=1` required. The API is not in Anthropic's public docs yet and may change between Claude Code releases — pin your Claude Code version in CI if you depend on this. Shell hooks continue to work unchanged and remain the stable option for simple allow/deny gates.

**Sources:** [Issue #91870: Mods — function hooks — GitHub](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/91870) · [Claude Code Function Hooks preview — claudefa.st](https://claudefa.st/blog/tools/hooks/function-hooks) · [I Tested Claude Code Function Hooks Before They Ship — Joe Njenga, Medium](https://medium.com/@joe.njenga/i-tested-claude-code-function-hooks-before-they-ship-and-built-my-first-one-2404158c00b0)

**The Anthropic Message Batches API sends up to 100,000 Claude requests in a single call at half the per-token price — the practical path for evaluation suites, document labeling, and content generation at scale.**

**When to reach for it.** Evaluations, data labeling, document classification, and content generation pipelines are the three places where teams burn most of their token budget. If the work doesn't need an answer right now — you can wait 30–90 minutes for a batch to finish — Anthropic reports a 50% discount on all input and output tokens.

**The pattern.** Each request in a batch carries a `custom_id` you set; results come back unordered, and `custom_id` is the only join key between your source data and the response.

```python
import anthropic, time

client = anthropic.Anthropic()

# 1. Submit (up to 100,000 requests per batch)
batch = client.messages.batches.create(requests=[
    {
        "custom_id": f"doc-{i}",
        "params": {
            "model": "claude-sonnet-5",
            "max_tokens": 256,
            "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": f"Classify sentiment: {text}"}],
        },
    }
    for i, text in enumerate(corpus)
])

# 2. Poll for completion
while batch.processing_status == "in_progress":
    time.sleep(60)
    batch = client.messages.batches.retrieve(batch.id)

# 3. Collect — results are unordered; custom_id is the join key
results = {}
for result in client.messages.batches.results(batch.id):
    if result.result.type == "succeeded":
        results[result.custom_id] = result.result.message.content[0].text
```

For large corpora, split into chunks of 50K–100K rows and submit parallel batches; chain them when the output of one job feeds the next.

**The limit that matters.** The processing window is 24 hours — a ceiling, not a target. Most batches finish in under an hour, but the API makes no tighter guarantee. Don't schedule a batch that a downstream step needs within the hour. Also: no streaming. If your evaluation requires partial results as they arrive, or you need the request-response chain for tracing, keep synchronous calls. The Batch API trades latency control for cost.

**Why it matters:** Swapping your synchronous eval loop for a batch call is a one-function change that immediately halves the token cost on every eval run — the most reliable cost optimization available at the API level.

**Sources:** [Message Batches API — Anthropic Docs](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/creating-message-batches) · [LLM Batch Processing with Anthropic SDK in production — DevOpsBoys](https://devopsboys.com/blog/llm-batch-processing-anthropic-sdk-production-2026) · [Batch inference cost tradeoffs — Chirag Hasija](https://chiraghasija.cc/posts/llm-batch-inference-cost-optimization-2026/)

**Build a TypeScript MCP server in 30 minutes and turn repetitive context-pasting into persistent, callable tools your agent finds automatically.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Give any MCP client — Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf — a named server with tools it calls and data it reads, so recurring questions are answered without pasting context every session.

Three things to know before writing a line of code:

- **MCP has three capability types.** *Tools* = functions Claude calls (`get_build_status`, `search_codebase`). *Resources* = data it reads (a file, a database row, a config). *Prompts* = reusable message templates. Most servers start with just tools.
- **Use stdio for local, Streamable HTTP for remote.** The HTTP+SSE transport from the 2024-era spec was deprecated on 2026-03-26. Local stdio servers are simpler to wire; anything deployed for a team needs Streamable HTTP.
- **Never use `console.log` in a stdio server.** The protocol uses stdout for JSON-RPC frames. A stray log line corrupts the framing silently. Use `console.error()` for debugging output.

**The 30-minute path:**

```bash
npm init -y
npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk zod
```

```typescript
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" });

server.tool(
  "get_build_status",
  "Returns current CI status for a branch",
  { branch: z.string().describe("branch name") },
  async ({ branch }) => ({
    content: [{ type: "text", text: `Status for ${branch}: passing` }]
  })
);

const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
```

Add to `claude_desktop_config.json` (or `~/.claude.json` for Claude Code):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-server": { "command": "node", "args": ["dist/index.js"] }
  }
}
```

Test tools before wiring to any client:

```bash
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector dist/index.js
```

MCP Inspector gives you a browser UI to invoke tools and inspect returned content — faster than debugging inside Claude.

**One real limit:** MCP has no built-in authentication. A stdio server only your account runs is safe. A remote Streamable HTTP server others can reach needs you to implement auth yourself — the 2026-07-28 spec outlines an OAuth 2.1 handshake, but the SDK does not enforce it. An unauthenticated public MCP server that can write to your systems is a privilege escalation surface. A [2026 arXiv taxonomy of MCP implementation faults](https://arxiv.org/html/2603.05637v1) found auth gaps as the most common class of real-world vulnerability. Build the tool before you expose it; decide who can call it before you deploy it.

**Sources:**
- [Build an MCP server — official quickstart](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/quickstart/server) (modelcontextprotocol.io)
- [TypeScript SDK v2](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk)
- [Real Faults in MCP Software: a Comprehensive Taxonomy](https://arxiv.org/html/2603.05637v1) — arXiv, independent analysis of implementation mistakes in production MCP servers

**Enable `bashEditDiffEnabled` and every Bash command that edits files appends a diff to its tool result — turning a silent write into an auditable record your hooks can act on.**

Claude Code v2.1.269 (Sept 11) ships an opt-in setting that solves a specific pain point in agentic runs: knowing what a Bash step actually changed on disk, without running a separate `git diff` after the fact.

**Enable it** in `.claude/settings.json` (project) or `~/.claude/settings.json` (global):
```json
{
  "bashEditDiffEnabled": true
}
```

Once set, any Bash command that writes to files — a `sed -i` patch, a build script that emits config, a generator writing output — appends the file diff to the tool result that Claude receives.

**The hook angle.** PostToolUse hooks bound to the `Bash` matcher receive the complete tool result as JSON, diff included. That makes a full file-change audit log straightforward:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [{
      "matcher": "Bash",
      "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "scripts/log-bash-edits.sh"}]
    }]
  }
}
```

Your `log-bash-edits.sh` receives the tool result on stdin, extracts the diff block, and appends it to a log file. During a long unattended run you can review exactly what changed, step by step.

You can also alert when the diff touches something it shouldn't — `.env`, dotfiles, or paths outside a project directory. PostToolUse hooks run after the fact; for a harder boundary that prevents the write, pair with a PreToolUse hook that pattern-matches the command string.

**The limit:** the diff appears only when Bash itself handles the file write. If Claude Code's own `Edit` or `Write` tools made the change, those go through separate hooks — no Bash diff is produced. The setting covers Bash-initiated edits only; `Edit`/`Write` tool calls need their own PostToolUse hooks if you want to log those too.

**Sources:** [Claude Code v2.1.269 changelog — releasebot.io](https://releasebot.io/updates/anthropic/claude-code) · [Hooks reference — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)
